==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|eagreness}} {{qualifier|obsolete}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|egernesse}}, {{m|enm|egrenesse}}; equivalent to {{suffix|en|eager|ness}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|GA}} {{IPA|en|/ˈiɡɚnəs/}}
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|en|/ˈiːɡənəs/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-eagerness.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{hyph|en|ea|ger|ness}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|-|+}}

# The state or quality of being [[eager]]; [[ardent]] [[desire]].
#* {{RQ:Burnett Secret Garden|20||The things he had to tell about...were enough to make you almost tremble with excitement, when you heard all the intimate details from an animal charmer and realized with what thrilling '''eagerness''' and anxiety the whole busy underworld was working.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} [[tartness|Tartness]], [[sourness]].
#* {{RQ:Pliny Holland Historie of the World|2|19|chapter= Of Garden herbes which serue for to season our meats|31|[[senvy|Senvie]], which is of a most biting and stinging tast, of a fierie effect,{{...}}serveth also to make a pretie dish of meat to be eaten, being boiled or stewed between too{{sic|two}} little dishes in some convenient liquor, in such sort, as a man shall not feele it to bite at the tongues end, nor complaine of any '''eagernesse''' that it hath.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1691|author=Thomas Tryon|title=The Way to Health, Long Life and Happiness{{...}}|edition=2nd|page=121|text=Presently the balsamick body is wounded, and turns sour sooner or later according to the degree of the motion; for this fierce motion or working wastes the pure ''spirituous Balsam'', and awakens the original Qualities of ''Saturn'' and ''Mars'', ''viz.'' an astringent '''Eagerness''', or sour hard Quality, that would not have been manifested, if this irregular Motion had not excited it.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1744|author=William Ellis|title=The London and Country Brewer|volume=2|edition=5th|page=148|text=To this I answer that Beer nor Ale can be drawed off so fine from  the Mash-Tun or Cooler, as to want a sufficient Sediment to feed it, as may be easily proved by an Observation when the Drink is drawn off, that never yet appeared to me without rather too much of this filthy Compound, which by its earthy and saline Parts naturally induces Hardness and '''Eagerness''' in the Liquor.}}

